   "text": "A home Wi-Fi network is like a digital highway that connects your laptop to the internet, allowing you to watch videos and access other online content. At the heart of this network is the router, which acts as the central hub. The router connects to your internet service provider (ISP) and distributes the internet connection to all the devices in your home that are connected to it, such as your laptop.\n\nThe router also serves as the access point for your Wi-Fi network. It broadcasts a wireless signal that your laptop can pick up, allowing it to connect to the internet without the need for cables. This wireless signal operates on specific radio channels, which are like lanes on a highway. These channels are part of the radio frequency spectrum and are used to transmit data between the router and your laptop.\n\nWhen you start streaming a video, the video data is broken down into small pieces called packets. These packets travel from the internet, through your ISP, and into your router. The router then sends these packets over the Wi-Fi signal to your laptop. Each packet contains a small part of the video, and your laptop reassembles these packets to play the video smoothly.\n\nHowever, in a home network, there can be multiple devices trying to use the Wi-Fi signal at the same time."
